.TH CBMC "1" "March 1, 2026" "cbmc-6.10.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cbmc \- Bounded Model Checker for C and C++ programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cbmc
.RI [ options ]
.IR file.c " ..."
.br
.B cbmc
.RI [ options ]
.I file.gb
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcbmc\fR verifies C and C++ programs using bounded model checking.  It
supports C89, C99, most of C11/C17/C23, and most compiler extensions
provided by \fBgcc\fR(1) and Visual Studio.
.PP
\fBcbmc\fR can verify array bounds (buffer overflows), pointer safety,
arithmetic overflow, user-specified assertions, and many other properties.
The verification is performed by unwinding loops in the program up to a
given bound, encoding the resulting program into a logical formula, and
passing it to a SAT or SMT decision procedure.
.PP
\fBcbmc\fR can read C/C++ source code directly, or a pre-compiled GOTO
binary generated by \fBgoto\-cc\fR(1).
.PP
Without further options, \fBcbmc\fR checks all properties (automatically
generated and user-specified) found in the program.  If any property can
be violated, a counterexample trace is printed.  The analysis can be
restricted to a particular property with \fB\-\-property\fR.
.PP
A typical workflow is to (1) compile source into a GOTO binary using
\fBgoto\-cc\fR(1), then (2) optionally perform instrumentation with
\fBgoto\-instrument\fR(1), and finally (3) run the analysis with \fBcbmc\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Standard Checks"
From version \fB6.0\fR onwards, \fBcbmc\fR, \fBgoto-analyzer\fR and some other
tools
apply some checks to the program by default (called the "standard checks"), with
the
aim to provide a better user experience for a non-expert user of the tool. These
checks are:
.TP
\fB\-\-pointer\-check\fR
enable pointer checks
.TP
\fB\-\-bounds\-check\fR
enable array bounds checks
.TP
\fB\-\-undefined\-shift\-check\fR
check shift greater than bit\-width
.TP
\fB\-\-div\-by\-zero\-check\fR
enable division by zero checks for integer division
.TP
\fB\-\-float\-div\-by\-zero\-check\fR
enable division by zero checks for floating-point division
.TP
\fB\-\-pointer\-primitive\-check\fR
checks that all pointers in pointer primitives are valid or null
.TP
\fB\-\-signed\-overflow\-check\fR
enable signed arithmetic over\- and underflow checks
.TP
\fB\-\-malloc\-may\-fail\fR
allow malloc calls to return a null pointer
.TP
\fB\-\-malloc\-fail\-null\fR
set malloc failure mode to return null
.TP
\fB\-\-unwinding\-assertions\fR
generate unwinding assertions (cannot be
used with \fB\-\-cover\fR)
.PP
These checks can all be deactivated at once by using the
\fB\-\-no\-standard\-checks\fR flag
like in the header example, or individually, by prepending a \fBno\-\fR before
the flag, like
so:
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pointer\-check\fR
disable pointer checks
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-bounds\-check\fR
disable array bounds checks
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-undefined\-shift\-check\fR
do not perform check for shift greater than bit\-width
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-div\-by\-zero\-check\fR
disable division by zero checks
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pointer\-primitive\-check\fR
do not check that all pointers in pointer primitives are valid or null
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-signed\-overflow\-check\fR
disable signed arithmetic over\- and underflow checks
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-malloc\-may\-fail\fR
do not allow malloc calls to fail by default
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-unwinding\-assertions\fR
do not generate unwinding assertions
.PP
If an already set flag is re-set, like calling \fB\-\-pointer\-check\fR
when default checks are already on, the flag is simply ignored.
.SS "Analysis options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-standard\-checks\fR
Disable all standard (default) checks.  Use this as a starting point when
you only want to enable specific checks manually.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-properties\fR
List all properties (assertions, checks) in the program without running
the analysis.
.TP
\fB\-\-symex\-coverage\-report\fR \fIfile\fR
Generate a Cobertura XML coverage report in \fIfile\fR, showing which
lines of code were reached during symbolic execution.
.TP
\fB\-\-property\fR \fIid\fR
Only check the property with the given \fIid\fR.  Use \fB\-\-show\-properties\fR
to list available property identifiers.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR
Print a counterexample trace for each failed property, showing the
sequence of assignments and function calls leading to the violation.
.TP
\fB\-\-stop\-on\-fail\fR
Stop the analysis as soon as the first property violation is found.
Implies \fB\-\-trace\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-localize\-faults\fR
Attempt to identify the most likely source of a property violation
(experimental).
.SS "C/C++ frontend options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-preprocess\fR
Stop after preprocessing and print the result to standard output.
.TP
\fB\-\-test\-preprocessor\fR
Stop after preprocessing; discard output.  Useful for checking whether
preprocessing succeeds.
.TP
\fB\-I\fR \fIpath\fR
Add \fIpath\fR to the include search path (C/C++).
.TP
\fB\-\-include\fR \fIfile\fR
Force inclusion of \fIfile\fR at the start of each translation unit (C/C++).
.TP
\fB\-D\fR \fImacro\fR[=\fIvalue\fR]
Define preprocessor macro \fImacro\fR, optionally with \fIvalue\fR (C/C++).
.TP
\fB\-\-c89\fR, \fB\-\-c99\fR, \fB\-\-c11\fR, \fB\-\-c17\fR, \fB\-\-c23\fR
Select the C language standard.  The default is \fBc11\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cpp98\fR, \fB\-\-cpp03\fR, \fB\-\-cpp11\fR
Select the C++ language standard.  The default is \fBcpp98\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-unsigned\-char\fR
Treat plain \fBchar\fR as unsigned by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-round\-to\-nearest\fR, \fB\-\-round\-to\-even\fR
Use round-to-nearest-even for floating-point arithmetic (default).
.TP
\fB\-\-round\-to\-plus\-inf\fR
Use round-towards-positive-infinity for floating-point arithmetic.
.TP
\fB\-\-round\-to\-minus\-inf\fR
Use round-towards-negative-infinity for floating-point arithmetic.
.TP
\fB\-\-round\-to\-zero\fR
Use round-towards-zero (truncation) for floating-point arithmetic.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-library\fR
Do not link the built-in abstract C library models.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-nondet\-tree\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Limit the depth of non-deterministically initialized object trees
(e.g., linked data structures) to \fIN\fR levels.  At level \fIN\fR,
all pointers are set to \fBNULL\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-null\-tree\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Set the minimum depth at which a pointer may first be \fBNULL\fR in a
recursively non-deterministically initialized structure.
.TP
\fB\-\-function\fR \fIname\fR
Use \fIname\fR as the program entry point instead of \fBmain\fR.
.SS "Platform options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-arch\fR \fIarch\fR
Set analysis architecture, which defaults to the host architecture. Use one of:
\fBalpha\fR, \fBarm\fR, \fBarm64\fR, \fBarmel\fR, \fBarmhf\fR, \fBhppa\fR,
\fBi386\fR, \fBia64\fR,
\fBmips\fR, \fBmips64\fR, \fBmips64el\fR, \fBmipsel\fR, \fBmipsn32\fR,
\fBmipsn32el\fR, \fBpowerpc\fR, \fBppc64\fR, \fBppc64le\fR, \fBriscv64\fR,
\fBs390\fR,
\fBs390x\fR, \fBsh4\fR, \fBsparc\fR, \fBsparc64\fR, \fBv850\fR, \fBx32\fR,
\fBx86_64\fR, or
\fBnone\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-os\fR \fIos\fR
Set analysis operating system, which defaults to the host operating system. Use
one of: \fBfreebsd\fR, \fBlinux\fR, \fBmacos\fR, \fBnetbsd\fR, \fBopenbsd\fR,
\fBsolaris\fR, \fBhurd\fR, or \fBwindows\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-i386\-linux\fR, \fB\-\-i386\-win32\fR, \fB\-\-i386\-macos\fR,
\fB\-\-ppc\-macos\fR, \fB\-\-win32\fR, \fB\-\-winx64\fR
Set analysis architecture and operating system.
.TP
\fB\-\-LP64\fR, \fB\-\-ILP64\fR, \fB\-\-LLP64\fR, \fB\-\-ILP32\fR,
\fB\-\-LP32\fR
Set width of int, long and pointers, but don't override default architecture and
operating system.
.TP
\fB\-\-16\fR, \fB\-\-32\fR, \fB\-\-64\fR
Equivalent to \fB\-\-LP32\fR, \fB\-\-ILP32\fR, \fB\-\-LP64\fR (on Windows:
\fB\-\-LLP64\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-little\-endian\fR
Assume little-endian byte order for word-byte conversions.
.TP
\fB\-\-big\-endian\fR
Assume big-endian byte order for word-byte conversions.
.TP
\fB\-\-gcc\fR
Use GCC as the C preprocessor.
.SS "Program representations:"
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-parse\-tree\fR
Display the parse tree of the input source code.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-symbol\-table\fR
Display the symbol table, listing all variables, functions, and types
with their attributes.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-goto\-functions\fR
Display the GOTO program instructions for all functions.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\-goto\-functions\fR
List the names of all functions in the GOTO program without showing
their bodies.
.TP
\fB\-\-validate\-goto\-model\fR
Run additional well-formedness checks on the GOTO program
representation.
.TP
\fB\-\-validate\-ssa\-equation\fR
Run additional well-formedness checks on the SSA (Static Single
Assignment) representation used during symbolic execution.
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-symex\-ready\-goto\fR \fIfilename\fR
Write the GOTO binary, after all preprocessing and instrumentation
passes, to \fIfilename\fR.  This binary can be loaded directly for
symbolic execution, skipping the front-end stages.
.SS "Program instrumentation options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-bounds\-check\fR
Insert assertions that array and buffer accesses are within bounds.
.TP
\fB\-\-pointer\-check\fR
Insert assertions that pointers are valid (not null, not dangling, and
within object bounds) when dereferenced.
.TP
\fB\-\-memory\-leak\-check\fR
Insert assertions that all dynamically allocated memory is freed before
program termination.
.TP
\fB\-\-memory\-cleanup\-check\fR
Insert assertions that all dynamically allocated memory is explicitly
freed before program termination (stricter than \fB\-\-memory\-leak\-check\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-div\-by\-zero\-check\fR
Insert assertions that integer division and modulo operations do not
divide by zero.
.TP
\fB\-\-float\-div\-by\-zero\-check\fR
Insert assertions that floating-point division does not divide by zero.
.TP
\fB\-\-signed\-overflow\-check\fR
Insert assertions that signed integer arithmetic does not overflow or
underflow (undefined behavior in C).
.TP
\fB\-\-unsigned\-overflow\-check\fR
Insert assertions that unsigned integer arithmetic does not wrap around.
Note that unsigned overflow is well-defined in C; this check is for
detecting unintended wrapping.
.TP
\fB\-\-pointer\-overflow\-check\fR
Insert assertions that pointer arithmetic does not overflow or
underflow.
.TP
\fB\-\-conversion\-check\fR
Insert assertions that values can be faithfully represented after a
type cast (e.g., no truncation or sign change).
.TP
\fB\-\-undefined\-shift\-check\fR
Insert assertions that shift amounts are non-negative and less than the
bit-width of the shifted type.
.TP
\fB\-\-float\-overflow\-check\fR
Insert assertions that floating-point operations do not produce
infinity (\(+-Inf).
.TP
\fB\-\-nan\-check\fR
Insert assertions that floating-point operations do not produce NaN.
.TP
\fB\-\-enum\-range\-check\fR
Insert assertions that enum-typed expressions hold values within the
defined range of the enum.
.TP
\fB\-\-pointer\-primitive\-check\fR
Insert assertions that all pointers passed to pointer primitives
(such as \fB__CPROVER_r_ok\fR) are valid or null.
.TP
\fB\-\-retain\-trivial\-checks\fR
Keep checks that are trivially true (e.g., constant expressions).
By default these are optimized away.
.TP
\fB\-\-error\-label\fR \fIlabel\fR
Insert an assertion that the given \fIlabel\fR is unreachable.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-built\-in\-assertions\fR
Ignore assertions in the built-in C library models.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-assertions\fR
Ignore all user-written \fBassert()\fR statements and
\fB__CPROVER_assert\fR calls.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-assumptions\fR
Ignore all \fB__CPROVER_assume\fR statements.
.TP
\fB\-\-assert\-to\-assume\fR
Convert all user assertions to assumptions.  This is useful for
restricting the analysis to a subset of properties.
.TP
\fB\-\-cover\fR \fIcriterion\fR
Generate coverage goals instead of checking properties.  \fIcriterion\fR
is one of: \fBassertion\fR, \fBassume\fR, \fBbranch\fR, \fBcondition\fR,
\fBcover\fR, \fBdecision\fR, \fBlocation\fR, or \fBmcdc\fR.
Cannot be used with \fB\-\-unwinding\-assertions\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cover\-failed\-assertions\fR
Do not stop coverage checking at failed assertions.  This is the
default when using \fB\-\-cover assertions\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-test\-suite\fR
Print the generated test suite for the coverage criterion.  Requires
\fB\-\-cover\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mm\fR \fIMM\fR
Set the memory consistency model for concurrent programs.  \fIMM\fR is
one of \fBsc\fR (sequential consistency, the default), \fBtso\fR, \fBpso\fR,
\fBrmo\fR, or \fBpower\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-malloc\-may\-fail\fR
Model \fBmalloc\fR as potentially returning a null pointer.
.TP
\fB\-\-malloc\-fail\-assert\fR
When \fBmalloc\fR fails, first assert false (to flag the failure), then
assume false (to block the path).
.TP
\fB\-\-malloc\-fail\-null\fR
When \fBmalloc\fR fails, return null (the default failure mode when
\fB\-\-malloc\-may\-fail\fR is enabled).
.TP
\fB\-\-string\-abstraction\fR
Track C string lengths and zero-termination (experimental).
.TP
\fB\-\-dfcc\-debug\-lib\fR
Enable debug assertions in the CProver contracts library.
.TP
\fB\-\-dfcc\-simple\-invalid\-pointer\-model\fR
Use a simplified invalid pointer model in the CProver contracts library.
Faster but potentially unsound.
.TP
\fB\-\-reachability\-slice\fR
Remove instructions that cannot appear on any trace from the entry
point to a property.  This can significantly reduce formula size.
.TP
\fB\-\-reachability\-slice\-fb\fR
Remove instructions that cannot appear on any trace from the entry
point \fIthrough\fR a property (forward-backward slicing).
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-slice\fR
Remove all instructions that do not affect any assertion (experimental;
see \fBcbmc\fR issue #260 for known soundness issues).
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\-unused\-functions\fR
Remove functions that are trivially unreachable from the entry point.
.SS "Semantic transformations:"
.TP
\fB\-\-nondet\-static\fR
Initialize all variables with static lifetime (global and static local
variables) to non-deterministic values instead of their declared
initializers.  This is useful for verifying code in isolation without
relying on specific initialization.
.SS "BMC options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-paths\fR [\fIstrategy\fR]
Explore execution paths one at a time instead of merging them.
\fIstrategy\fR selects the path exploration order; use
\fB\-\-show\-symex\-strategies\fR to list available strategies.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-symex\-strategies\fR
List available path exploration strategies for use with \fB\-\-paths\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-goto\-symex\-steps\fR
Print each step that symbolic execution takes, including diagnostic
information about the call stack and guard size.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-points\-to\-sets\fR
Print points-to sets computed during pointer dereference resolution.
Requires \fB\-\-json\-ui\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-program\-only\fR
Print the program expression (the SSA equations) without solving.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-byte\-ops\fR
Print all byte-extract and byte-update operations in the formula.
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR nr
limit search depth
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-field\-sensitivity\-array\-size\fR \fIM\fR
Apply field sensitivity (tracking individual elements) to arrays of at
most \fIM\fR elements.  The default is 64.  Larger values increase
precision but may increase memory usage and solving time.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-array\-field\-sensitivity\fR
Disable field sensitivity for arrays entirely.  Equivalent to
\fB\-\-max\-field\-sensitivity\-array\-size\fR \fI0\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-loops\fR
List all loops in the program with their identifiers.  Use this to
obtain loop IDs for \fB\-\-unwindset\fR and \fB\-\-incremental\-loop\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-unwind\fR \fInr\fR
Unwind all loops at most \fInr\fR times.  Without
\fB\-\-unwinding\-assertions\fR, this may yield unsound results if the
bound is insufficient.
.TP
\fB\-\-unwindset\fR [\fIT\fR:]\fIL\fR:\fIB\fR,...
Set a per-loop unwinding bound: unwind loop \fIL\fR at most \fIB\fR times,
optionally restricted to thread \fIT\fR.  Overrides \fB\-\-unwind\fR for the
specified loops.  Use \fB\-\-show\-loops\fR to obtain loop IDs.
.TP
\fB\-\-incremental\-loop\fR \fIL\fR
Check properties after each unwinding of loop \fIL\fR.  Use
\fB\-\-show\-loops\fR to obtain the loop ID.
.TP
\fB\-\-unwind\-min\fR \fInr\fR
When using \fB\-\-incremental\-loop\fR, start checking properties after
\fInr\fR unwindings.  A value of 0 or 1 means all properties are checked
from the first iteration.
.TP
\fB\-\-unwind\-max\fR \fInr\fR
When using \fB\-\-incremental\-loop\fR, stop after \fInr\fR unwindings.
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-properties\-before\-unwind\-min\fR
When using \fB\-\-incremental\-loop\fR, do not check properties before
\fB\-\-unwind\-min\fR is reached.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-vcc\fR
Print the verification conditions (logical formulas) without solving
them.
.TP
\fB\-\-slice\-formula\fR
Remove assignments from the formula that do not affect any property.
This can significantly reduce solving time.
.TP
\fB\-\-unwinding\-assertions\fR
Generate assertions that check whether the loop unwinding bound is
sufficient.  If any unwinding assertion fails, the bound was too small
and the verification result may be unsound.  Enabled by default; cannot
be used with \fB\-\-cover\fR.  Overrides \fB\-\-no\-unwinding\-assertions\fR
when both are given.
.TP
\fB\-\-partial\-loops\fR
Permit execution paths that exit a loop before the loop condition
becomes false (i.e., paths that only partially execute the loop body).
Such paths may be spurious, potentially resulting in false alarms.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-self\-loops\-to\-assumptions\fR
Do not simplify self-loops such as \fBwhile(1){}\fR to
\fBassume(false)\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-symex\-complexity\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Abandon execution paths whose complexity (currently measured by guard
size) exceeds \fIN\fR.  This can prevent state-space explosion at the
cost of potentially missing bugs on complex paths.
.TP
\fB\-\-symex\-complexity\-failed\-child\-loops\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
When using \fB\-\-symex\-complexity\-limit\fR, blacklist a loop after
\fIN\fR of its child branches have been abandoned due to complexity
violations.
.TP
\fB\-\-graphml\-witness\fR \fIfilename\fR
Write a verification witness in GraphML format to \fIfilename\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-symex\-cache\-dereferences\fR
Cache the results of repeated pointer dereferences during symbolic
execution.  This can improve performance when the same pointer is
dereferenced multiple times.
.SS "Backend options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-object\-bits\fR \fIn\fR
Set the number of bits used for object addresses in the pointer model.
A higher value allows more distinct objects but increases formula size.
.TP
\fB\-\-sat\-solver\fR \fIsolver\fR
Use the specified SAT solver (e.g., \fBminisat\fR, \fBcadical\fR,
\fBglucose\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-external\-sat\-solver\fR \fIcmd\fR
Invoke \fIcmd\fR as an external SAT solver process.  The solver must
accept DIMACS CNF on standard input.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-sat\-preprocessor\fR
Disable the SAT solver's built-in simplifier/preprocessor.
.TP
\fB\-\-dimacs\fR
Output the formula in DIMACS CNF format instead of solving it.
.TP
\fB\-\-beautify\fR
Attempt to produce a more readable counterexample by using a greedy
heuristic to minimize variable values.
.TP
\fB\-\-smt2\fR
Use the default SMT2 solver (Z3).
.TP
\fB\-\-bitwuzla\fR
Use Bitwuzla as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-boolector\fR
Use Boolector as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-cprover\-smt2\fR
Use the CProver built-in SMT2 solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-cvc5\fR
Use CVC5 as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-mathsat\fR
Use MathSAT as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-yices\fR
Use Yices as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-z3\fR
Use Z3 as the SMT solver.
.TP
\fB\-\-fpa\fR
Use the theory of floating-point arithmetic in the SMT encoding
(instead of bit-blasting floating-point operations).
.TP
\fB\-\-external\-smt2\-solver\fR \fIcmd\fR
Invoke \fIcmd\fR as an external SMT2 solver process.  Combine with one
of the solver selection options (e.g., \fB\-\-z3\fR) for solver-specific
formula constraints.
.TP
\fB\-\-refine\fR
Use counterexample-guided abstraction refinement (CEGAR) for both
arrays and arithmetic (experimental).
.TP
\fB\-\-refine\-arrays\fR
Use abstraction refinement for arrays only.
.TP
\fB\-\-refine\-arithmetic\fR
Use abstraction refinement for arithmetic expressions only.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-node\-refinement\fR \fIN\fR
Limit the number of refinement iterations for arithmetic expressions
to \fIN\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-incremental\-smt2\-solver\fR \fIcmd\fR
Use the incremental SMT backend, invoking \fIcmd\fR as the solver.
The solver must accept incremental SMTlib v2.6 input on standard input
and support the QF_AUFBV logic.
.br
Example: \fB\-\-incremental\-smt2\-solver\fR \fI'z3 \-smt2 \-in'\fR
.br
Example:
\fB\-\-incremental\-smt2\-solver\fR \fI'cvc5 \-\-lang=smtlib2.6
\-\-incremental'\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-outfile\fR \fIfilename\fR
Write the generated formula to \fIfilename\fR instead of solving it.
.TP
\fB\-\-dump\-smt\-formula\fR \fIfilename\fR
Write the SMT formula (in incremental SMTlib format) to \fIfilename\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-write\-solver\-stats\-to\fR \fIjson\-file\fR
Write solver query complexity statistics to \fIjson\-file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-refine\-strings\fR
Use string refinement for string operations (experimental).
.TP
\fB\-\-string\-printable\fR
Restrict non-deterministic strings to printable characters
(experimental).
.TP
\fB\-\-arrays\-uf\-never\fR
Never encode arrays as uninterpreted functions; always use the theory
of arrays.
.TP
\fB\-\-arrays\-uf\-always\fR
Always encode arrays as uninterpreted functions.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-array\-constraints\fR
Print array theory constraints added during post-processing.  Requires
\fB\-\-json\-ui\fR.
.SS "User-interface options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-xml\-ui\fR
Produce output in XML format.
.TP
\fB\-\-xml\-interface\fR
Enable the bi-directional XML interface for tool integration.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-ui\fR
Produce output in JSON format.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-interface\fR
Enable the bi-directional JSON interface for tool integration.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\-json\-extended\fR
Include the \fBrawLhs\fR property in JSON trace output.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\-show\-function\-calls\fR
Include function call and return events in plain-text traces.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\-show\-code\fR
Include the original source code in plain-text traces.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\-hex\fR
Display values in hexadecimal in plain-text traces.
.TP
\fB\-\-compact\-trace\fR
Produce a compact counterexample trace (omitting unchanged variables).
.TP
\fB\-\-stack\-trace\fR
Produce only a stack trace (no variable assignments) for
counterexamples.
.TP
\fB\-\-flush\fR
Flush every line of output immediately.  Useful when piping output to
another process.
.TP
\fB\-\-verbosity\fR \fIN\fR
Set the verbosity level to \fIN\fR.  The default is 6.  Level 0 suppresses
all output; higher values produce more diagnostic information.
.TP
\fB\-\-timestamp\fR [\fBmonotonic\fR|\fBwall\fR]
Print microsecond-precision timestamps with each message.
\fBmonotonic\fR: timestamps increase monotonically from program start.
\fBwall\fR: ISO\-8601 wall-clock timestamps.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
All CProver tools honor this variable when creating temporary files and
directories.
.PP
The C preprocessor invoked by \fBcbmc\fR will use its standard environment
variables (such as \fBCPATH\fR and \fBC_INCLUDE_PATH\fR) to locate header
files.
.SH EXAMPLES
Verify a C source file with all default checks enabled:
.IP
.B cbmc file.c
.PP
Verify with a loop unwinding bound of 10 (unwinding assertions, which are
enabled by default, will flag if the bound is insufficient):
.IP
.B cbmc \-\-unwind 10 file.c
.PP
Check only a specific property and print a counterexample trace on failure:
.IP
.B cbmc \-\-property main.assertion.1 \-\-trace file.c
.PP
List all properties in a program without running the analysis:
.IP
.B cbmc \-\-show\-properties file.c
.PP
Use a pre-compiled GOTO binary (useful for larger projects):
.RS
.nf
goto\-cc \-o program.gb program.c
cbmc program.gb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Full pipeline with instrumentation:
.RS
.nf
goto\-cc \-o program.gb program.c
goto\-instrument \-\-bounds\-check program.gb program\-instr.gb
cbmc program\-instr.gb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use an SMT solver instead of the default SAT solver:
.IP
.B cbmc \-\-z3 file.c
.PP
Generate coverage goals:
.IP
.B cbmc \-\-cover branch \-\-no\-standard\-checks file.c
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All properties verified successfully (\fBVERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL\fR).
.TP
.B 1
Usage error: invalid or conflicting command line options.
.TP
.B 2
Parse error: an input source file could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 6
An internal error or unhandled exception occurred.
.TP
.B 10
One or more properties were violated (\fBVERIFICATION FAILED\fR).
